  "overview_markdown": "ZeroClaw is a Rust-based agent runtime distributed as a single binary, designed around the philosophy that you own the agent, the data, and the machine. It connects to ~20 LLM providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Ollama, and more), reaches users through 30+ channels (Discord, Telegram, Matrix, WeCom, email, voice, webhooks), and acts via tools including shell, browser, HTTP, hardware, and MCP servers.\n\nIts standout trait versus OpenClaw is an obsessive security posture visible in the commit history: a shared network egress guard with pinned destinations, NAT64-aware metadata address blocking, per-instance plugin dial budgets, machine trust-store TLS for plugins, and hardened credential caches. Plugins run under a host-owned egress policy with WASI HTTP interception, making it one of the most network-isolated agent runtimes in the ecosystem.\n\nCompared to OpenClaw, ZeroClaw trades ecosystem breadth for leanness and auditability. It boots in milliseconds, uses a fraction of the memory, and runs on virtually any platform — but it lacks OpenClaw's massive skill registry, dashboard UI, and community scale. It is best suited for technical users who want a minimal, hardened, self-hosted agent core they can fully audit.",
